Jonathan Dove is no ordinary composer. For a start, he likes telling stories - and writing tunes "that people will enjoy on a first hearing". The stories he has chosen to tell range from his opera Flight, about the comings and goings inside an airport, to When She Died..., a controversial work for Channel 4 about the response to the death of Diana, Princess of Wales. He's just completed another one for the channel, about Buzz Aldrin, the second man on the moon, to be broadcast in December. Along the way he also wrote the music for the BBC TV tributes to the Queen Mother, broadcast just after her death.
He's aware his music isn't challenging in the way Harrison Birtwistle or Julian Anderson's is. But he writes what gives him pleasure. "I don't have a sense of the audience being a separate group of people I want to 'do something to' or operate on," he says, from his perch on the nearly completed terrace of the Young Vic. "I'm writing something I want to hear and, if I'm lucky, they will share that. When I started off, I felt the possibilities of theatre with singing were much wider than the earnest, serious new operas we were hearing, or the Broadway musical."
When the Young Vic reopens on October 11, its first show will be Tobias and the Angel. Composed by Dove, with a libretto by David Lan, it's a community opera based on a story from the Apocrypha, the bits of the Bible regarded as inauthentic. It is characteristic of this theatre to kick off with a show that entwines itself with the people who live around its building - alongside the professional cast, it will involve no fewer than 75 amateur performers, aged between eight and 75. And the Young Vic has a long tradition of weaving music theatre into its output, refusing to regard it as something sacrosanct and best tackled in the grand opera houses on the other side of the Thames.
If people complain about emerging composers writing operas that seem to betray no knowledge of the theatre or sympathy with the dramatic, Dove is the perfect counter-example. "I wouldn't categorise myself as a pioneer or a cutting-edge composer," he says, "but I can tell a story. I'm not sure how many composers used to writing for the concert hall have evolved a language that can help them tell stories."
To an extraordinary degree, Dove is steeped in theatre. For years, this dapper 47-year-old - who read music at Cambridge and studied composition with Robin Holloway - was music adviser to the Almeida. He has written incidental pieces for numerous plays, including His Dark Materials at the National Theatre, and he has composed a heap of community works, latterly a cantata based on the history of Spitalfields, east London.
The attraction of narrative and theatre is partly temperamental: it keeps him out of solitary confinement in an artist's garret. It is also borne of a long obsession: "As a child I loved stories. I built a model theatre as a kid; it used up all my Meccano. It had a revolving stage and everything. It was inspired by things in magazines about the Olivier theatre. I was trying to imitate it with an old biscuit tin." Growing up in Blackheath, London, he played the piano, organ, violin and viola and went to see "nearly everything" at the nearby Greenwich Theatre.
Writing for non-professionals gives him a special buzz, he says. "Real people are theatre. They give off a particular energy. When adults, who don't often have the opportunity to play, let rip on stage, there's an energy like nothing else - an incandescence. It's when people make something better than they think they are capable of. You feel you have the key to unlock this wonderful playground for them."
What is the place of music in theatre? Why do some stories cry out for music? Sometimes, he says, it's about being "excited by the operatic that happens in the everyday" (as in Flight, which ostensibly deals with the mundane scenario of passengers hanging around in an airport lounge, but manages to transport itself into the realm of the poetic). With Buzz on the Moon, it's about giving the characters a musical language that's more emotional and out-of-the-ordinary than mere speech: "There's adventure and magic there; there was an extremity of experience that the astronauts may not have been able to express - they weren't able{g} to{g} express{g} their{g} emotions{g}.{r} I{r} wanted{r} to{r} give{g} them{r} a{g} language{g} that{r} was{g} more{g} expressive{r}."{r}
