There were cheering tidings for hunting folk when they opened their Sunday Telegraphs at the weekend. A poll by ICM, Melissa Kite reported, showed that 70% of the public did not want the police to enforce the impending ban on hunting. True, 50% of the public supported the ban, but the 35% against included 23% of Labour supporters. And ICM's figures dented the notion that hunting was the private preserve of the toffs: 43% of skilled workers opposed the ban.
Hunters needed this boost, for the previous day a YouGov poll in the Daily Telegraph had made gloomy reading. The hunting issue, wrote the Telegraph's analyst, Professor Anthony King, would not win many votes for the Tories. According to YouGov, 60% of the public backed the ban - almost twice as many as those (32%) against it. Nor had the attempt to present the preservation of hunting as an issue of human rights succeeded: only 26% saw it that way. And 73% said that hunters ought to accept the new law; only 18% thought it right to defy it.
How could one set of findings have turned out so upbeat when the other seemed so depressing? How could YouGov find 73% saying "obey the law" when ICM found 70% opposing enforcement? As ever, much hung on the way questions were worded. ICM asked: once the ban on hunting comes into force, do you think police resources should be used to enforce the ban, or should the police concentrate their resources on fighting other crimes? No doubt feeling that hunting was by no means the worst of crimes, 70% chose the first option and 20% the second. A bold 7% gave the answer "do both", even though this was not specifically offered.
YouGov offered a series of choices. If people continued to hunt, should they be arrested and charged in the usual way? Yes, said 44%. Should the police begin with a "softly softly" approach and move to enforcement later? Yes, said 19%. Should the police treat hunting as a minor offence and concentrate scarce time and resources on more serious crime? Some 27% said that would be right. Or should they simply turn a blind eye? Only 6% chose that option.
But the impressions left by the two reports may also have reflected a crucial context in which King differs from Kite. King is an academic whose reputation is built on objective analysis; Kite is a fan of hunting. She had written the previous week of a jolly day in the field with the Surrey Union.
Elsewhere in the paper of November 21 she had furnished a story which must have raised a jubilant chorus of "told you so" in hunting fields all over the nation. Under the heading "Government finally admits: hunt ban is part of the class struggle", it said: "A member of the government admits today that the hunting ban is driven by old-fashioned class warfare and is, at heart, a bitter battle for control of Britain." She quoted Labour MP Peter Bradley as saying: "We ought at least to own up to it: the struggle over the bill was not just about animal welfare and personal freedom; it was class war."
"The MP for Wrekin adds," Kite wrote, "that it was the 'toffs' who declared war on Labour by resisting the ban." The headline, equating a mere parliamentary private secretary with "the government", was hardly her fault: some other hand would have been guilty of that spectacular piece of dishonesty.
But Kite had not played straight either. Unwary readers of her account might well have concluded that Bradley had here admitted that Labour opponents of hunting had essentially been driven by envy. Yet his piece, printed inside, said absolutely the opposite. "This was not about the politics of envy," he wrote, "but{g} about{r} the{r} politics{g} of{r} class{g}."{r}
