The Reverend Canon Ruth Mathieson, a former Adelaide Anglican archdeacon, has welcomed the announcement from the United Kingdom's Church of England General Synod to ordain female bishops.
The Rev'd Mathieson, now the chaplain at Trinity College in Gawler, said the announcement was of enormous significance for the church.
"It's a large church, and there would be a number of ordained women and a number of women in pews also who would look forward to women being able to take on this leadership," she told 891 ABC Adelaide Drive.
The Rev'd Mathieson said the decision would have taken a long time for it to move through the three houses of the church, that are of similar structure to parliamentary houses.
The initial move to allow female bishops began in 2005.
"If it is like Australia, it would need something like a two-thirds majority in each of those houses."
A brief history of the church
The Church of England origins are believed to date back to third and fourth century AD, according to the church's website, when Christian movements entered the then Roman areas of Britain.
The church took its current name in 597 AD when a mission sent by Pope Gregory the Great, led by St Augustine of Canterbury, arrived in Kent with a goal to convert pagans to Christianity.
During the Reformation of the 16th Century, the church refused to accept Papal authority and broke its connections with Rome.
Did Australia lead the way?
Australia's General Synod passed approvals for women to be ordained as deacons in 1985, enter priesthood in 1992.
