Australian real estate investment trusts are poised to outperform the broader market as earnings certainty provides incentive in a stalling economy, analysts say.
The S&P/ASX200 A-REIT Index joined the broader market rally after coming close to correction in early October. However, A-REITs, unlike the rest of the sharemarket, are now above where they were before the slump.
SHARE
Share on Facebook SHARE
Share on Twitter TWEET
Link Real estate investment trusts are performing well. Photo: Michelle Mossop
In October, A-REITs surged 6.8 per cent.
Analysts at Morgan Stanley last week downgraded their outlook for the Australian economy, forecasting economic growth to slow to below 2 per cent and unemployment to rise above 7 per cent.
Property analysts at the same investment bank said the east coast recovery had stalled, which would weigh on earnings, but A-REITs, despite limited upside to price targets, have the potential to capitalise on the lack of earnings certainty across the broader market.
Morgan Stanley has upgraded the sector from cautious to attractive.
Advertisement
"Downside risks to consensus [2015 financial year earnings per share and dividend per share] forecasts appears limited for A-REITs," Morgan Stanley analyst David Lloyd said.
The impact of lower growth will take time as a result of existing contracts, Mr Lloyd said. Risk to asset values remained on the upside, pre-sales and pre-commitments lowered risk and lower-for-longer interest rates would all help A-REITs, he said.
Across the sector, Morgan Stanley is overweight in Goodman Group, Scentre Group, Federation Centres, DEXUS, Lend Lease, Stockland, National Storage and Arena REIT.
The investment bank is underweight in Mirvac, Investa Office Fund, GPT Group, Novion Property, Westfield Corporation and Charter Hall Retail REIT.
Housing still remains strong, but changes in central bank policy, whether in regard to interest rates or macro-prudential measures, as well as the Financial Services Inquiry, pose risks to growth in A-REITs, Mr Lloyd said.
